WOW-BG3 benilla patch 0090: BG3's see-through, ported from its own wall materials.

BG3 cuts the geometry between its camera and the controlled character away, so the character is
never hidden by a tree, a hut or a roof (open beta feedback: "when something stands between the
camera and the player ... that circular see-through window BG3 draws"). In world mode the
occluding geometry is WoW's and benilla draws it, so the cut is made here, with BG3's own law.

Where BG3 does it: in every wall's own material shader. `Base_ST_FOR` and kin in `Materials.pak`
(read with this repository's `release/lspk.py` and a SPIR-V disassembler; the Vulkan build
is stripped, the DX12 build keeps the names `g_SeeThroughPosition0`, `g_LocalCoverageScalar`,
`_FadeOpacity`) read an engine block (set 0, binding 6): two capsules, each from one shared point to
a target with a size `R`, and the materials' `SeeThroughEnabled` switch. They bind one texture by
id, `9dc5807b-3697-4b8f-ab0f-b9c5c1bd7010`, which is `REF_Cloud_B` (512 x 512 cloud noise, BC1, in
`Textures.pak`). Per fragment, line for line:
  n = 2 * cloud(uv * 2).r - 1
  for each capsule: q = the closest point of the segment to the fragment, clamped to the segment's
      box; v = clamp(max(distance(fragment, q) + 0.5 * n, 0) / R, 0, 1)
  discard where min(v0, v1) * k - 0.5 * k < 0   (k the object's own see-through scalar)
Seen down the line of sight a capsule is the "circle" round her; because the noise is fixed to the
wall's own texture coordinates while the capsule moves with her, its edge eats into the wall in
ragged strokes that change as she or the camera moves. That is the whole effect: no dither, no
blend, no mask pass. (The 4x4 Bayer stencil pattern BG3 also draws every frame, FadePatternOnePass,
is a different fade and is not this.)

How here: the same law in WoW's own world shaders, `wow_cut` in
- static_gx.wgsl, the retained static pass: ADT doodads (trees, rocks, fences), WMO groups
  (buildings, caves) and interior props. Most of the cut happens here.
- terrain.wgsl: hills and cliffs, behind a switch that is off (a hill is the eye clearance's).
- wow_model.wgsl, WMO batches alone (`model_flags.x`): a building's env-mapped and blended pieces,
  which static_gx declines. Units, the hero and her party come through the same shader and are
  never cut, which that flag settles without a per-mesh flag of its own.
The shared point is the eye. The one change: BG3 opts each object in (`CanSeeThrough` in its level
data), so its floors are never cut; WoW draws floors and walls with one shader, so a fragment is cut
only when it stands between the camera and her, past `near` on two tests: toward the eye along the
line to her (the floor at and in front of her feet stays) and in front of her on the ground plane,
toward the camera (nothing behind her opens, at any height; with the first test alone a camera
looking down steeply opened the top of the wall behind her).

The capsules ride the shared light buffer, rows 21 to 24 (LIGHT_HEADER_ROWS 21 -> 25): two targets
with their `R`, the eye with the noise's reach, and the noise's place, the margin, the noise's uv
scale and the terrain switch. Only the three shaders above mirror those rows; liquid.wgsl, wdl.wgsl
and wow_effect.wgsl stop before row 20 and are untouched. The eye row is also the gate that keeps a
view drawn from somewhere else (a portrait booth, the minimap bake, the login backdrop) out of the
cut, since they share the buffer. The noise is the buffer's new last region (one f32 a texel,
uploaded once), reached by each shader from the start of its own struct's last array
(`WOW_SEE_TAIL`, checked against the regions by a test in global_light.rs).

The noise comes from the player's own BG3: `$WOW_BG3_LINK_DIR/see-noise.r8`, REF_Cloud_B's red
channel as 512 x 512 bytes, which the mod's tools make from BG3's data (nothing of BG3's ships with
benilla). Until it is there the edge is clean.

Where it points (wowbg3_see.rs): from the eye to BG3's character from `tav.bin`, at chest height,
the point the plugin's own eye clearance measures from. The second capsule can follow what the
camera looks at (its forward ray crossing her height plane), for a fight where BG3's camera leaves
her for whoever's turn it is, as BG3's second capsule does; off by default. `R` is 4.4 yd: BG3's own
value is written by its engine straight into GPU memory and could not be read, so it is matched by
eye to BG3's frames. Tune it live in `$WOW_BG3_LINK_DIR/seethrough.txt` (`radius=`, `screen=`,
`near=`, `amp=`, `scale=`, `look=`, `apart=`, `terrain=`, `on=`), re-read twice a second, or with
`WOW_BG3_SEE_RADIUS=` and friends; `WOW_BG3_SEE=0` switches it off. Every size is 0 without world
mode, and the shaders then take their old path exactly.

The room behind the cut, and the street behind it: wmo_portal floods the portal PVS a second time
from where she stands when she is inside a building, and unions it into the visible set, so a cut
through a wall from outside shows the room instead of the sky; and while the cut is live the
exterior is drawn unrestricted (the outside leg's walk) even with the camera in a room, since the
cut can open any of its walls, so a cut from inside shows the street instead of the sky.

